ment": 7.38, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.65}, "rating_margin": 9.6, "sigma_race": 4.8, "sigma_total": 5.64, "baseline_margin": 9.58, "analyst_adjustment": -0.08, "key_factors": ["Cooper leads every recent poll by 5-14 points, including surveys from Republican-leaning pollsters", "Cooper's fundraising dominance: $47.2M raised and $12.5M on hand versus Whatley's $13.4M and $0.9M", "Whatley's campaign paused television advertising in mid-October while trailing", "Open seat in a mildly Republican-leaning state, with forecasters at Likely D (flip) or better"], "rationale": "Every poll since late September shows Cooper ahead by five to fourteen points, even in Republican-sponsored surveys, and he holds a decisive financial edge while Whatley has pulled his ads. North Carolina's slight Republican tilt is the main reason the race is not rated even more strongly.", "watch": "A late outside-spending surge on Whatley's behalf or a sharp national shift toward Republicans could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's Senate seat is open after Thom Tillis chose not to run again, setting up a contest between former Democratic Governor Roy Cooper and Republican national party chairman Michael Whatley. Polling has been consistent: every survey since late September shows Cooper ahead by five to fourteen points, including Republican-sponsored polls, and averages place his lead near ten points.\n\nCooper also holds a wide financial edge, raising about $47 million to Whatley's $13 million, and entered the final weeks with far more cash on hand. Whatley's campaign paused its television advertising in mid-October while behind. Rating outlets broadly call the race Likely Democratic, and betting markets give Cooper roughly a 95 percent chance. The state's slight Republican lean leaves a narrow path for an upset.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2